---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_TITLE, RELATIONS, pageHref } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base title={`Method · ${SITE_NAME}`}>
  <header class="wrap site-head">
    <a class="site-name" href={pageHref()}>{SITE_NAME}</a>
    <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Site">
      <a href={pageHref()}>All movements and words</a>
      <a href={pageHref('about')}>About</a>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main id="main" class="wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Method</h1>
      <h2>What counts as a movement</h2>
      <p>A movement, or ism, is mapped here when it passes three tests. It is a whole view of AI: what AI is, where it is heading and what we should do about it. It was born in or against the tech world, among people who build, fund, study or oppose the AI industry. And its own people use its name, with founding texts, a vocabulary and institutions that can be traced.</p>
      <p>Two movements in one family differ in what they value or what they fear. A strand shares its movement's demand and fears but may ground its values differently; effective accelerationism, for example, is a strand of techno-optimism. Movements sit next to the ones they share most people with.</p>
      <h2>The four lenses</h2>
      <p>Every movement page answers the same questions, in four lenses. <strong>Values</strong> and <strong>Fears</strong> are stated by the movement itself, in terms shared across the map, so that one movement's value can be seen as another's fear. <strong>Promises</strong> gives the order of society it aims at and the older tradition it echoes.</p>
      <p>Under Promises, the line that begins “If its demands became law tomorrow” is the site's own reasoning, not a quote. It follows the movement's own demands one step only: the first consequences, as the movement's own logic would predict them.</p>
      <p><strong>Interests</strong> starts with the funding: public records only, dated, with amounts where they are public, and organisations and named public funders only, never private giving. Grants to a movement's organisations are kept apart from investment in companies, and where no funding was found, the page says so.</p>
      <p>Then, after “Its demands could serve”, the site's reasoning again: who stands to gain, who would lose, and the opponents who could gain anyway. It names interest, never motive. A group could see a benefit; it is never said to be behind a movement, and each benefit comes with the mechanism that produces it. Funders are named only in the funding part, so the page never says that a funder gains. The same test and the same seven kinds of actor apply to all twelve movements.</p>
      <p>This is how the site critiques without critics: each movement is taken at its word and followed one step, and the reader sees the gap between what it promises and what would happen first, and between who it says it is for and who could gain from it.</p>
      <h2>The history of AI</h2>
      <p>The history of AI is the ground all twelve movements stand on: the machines, ideas, laboratories, money and laws. It runs newest first, in eras, back to the oldest documented root. Each moment is tagged with the movements it shaped and the key words it belongs to, and each era shows which movements were named in it and which words were coined.</p>
      <h2>Families, key words and the vocabulary</h2>
      <p>The twelve movements are grouped in six families, each named for what it wants done: transcend, accelerate, prevent, protect, reclaim and doubt. Below them are the words the debate keeps returning to. Every word is in the vocabulary, with what it means and who coined it, and when. A few are key words, with a page of their own that compares how the movements see them. Criticism is not a separate section. On a movement page it comes from the lenses, not from quoted critics; a key-word page still says who disputes the word.</p>
      <h2>People</h2>
      <p>A person is on the People page when the site names them for what they did: among a movement's notable figures, in a moment of the history, or as the coiner of a word. Public roles and documented actions only; no private individuals and no minors.</p>
      <p>What the page shows about each person is worked out from those entries, which are checked. Roles, labs, money, departures and connections are added only once a second reader has checked each against its source.</p>
      <p>The selection leans towards the United States and Britain, where much of this debate has been loudest. It will widen to China, continental Europe, Africa, India and Latin America.</p>
      <h2>How roots are marked</h2>
      <p>Every root in a timeline says how it is connected to the ism today. The most important difference is between ancestors the movement claims for itself and ancestors that historians attribute to it.</p>
      <ul>
        {Object.values(RELATIONS).map((r) => <li>{r}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Every entry names its source. Texts are first drafted, then checked claim by claim against those sources by a second reader. Entries not yet checked are marked as drafts.</p>
      <h2>Images</h2>
      <p>All images are made by people, never generated by AI. Each names its source and its rights. Images whose rights are not yet cleared are shown as labelled placeholders. Portraits are shown in black and white so that paintings and photographs sit together; each credits its photographer or artist and its source.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
